With the partitions set up, we can proceed to encryption. In general, all
partitions on a system should ideally be encrypted. The exception is the boot
partition (or partitions if you are using an XBOOTLDR partition) because the
UEFI firmware cannot read an encrypted EFI system partition. While GRUB does
support reading from an encrypted boot partition, this approach is generally
discouraged as it adds another layer of security, increases complexity and is
more difficult to maintain. For this reason, we will only encrypt our root
partition.

Afterwards, the root partition will be unlocked so it can be used in the next
steps.
